<h1 class="doc-title">UNIVERSAL QLIK-TO-POWER BI MIGRATION ENGINE</h1>
<p class="doc-subtitle">Technical Architecture, System Specifications &amp; End-to-End Migration Guide</p>
<p class="doc-meta">August 2026 | Microsoft Fabric PBIR 4.0 Compliant | Zero Hardcoding Guarantee</p>

<hr>

<h1>1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY &amp; OVERVIEW</h1>
<p>
The <strong>Universal Qlik-to-Power BI Migration Engine</strong> is an enterprise-grade autonomous migration system designed to convert any Qlik Sense or QlikView application file (<code>.qvf</code>) into a fully compliant Microsoft Fabric Power BI Project (<code>.pbip</code>) or Power BI Template (<code>.pbit</code>) with zero human intervention.
</p>

<div class="highlight-box">
    <strong>Core Value Proposition &amp; Enterprise Guarantees:</strong>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Zero Hardcoding Guarantee:</strong> The engine operates 100% dynamically. It does not rely on pre-coded column names, static table structures, or hardcoded visual layouts. All schemas are discovered on-the-fly.</li>
        <li><strong>Universal <code>.QVF</code> Compatibility:</strong> Whether migrating Financial Reports, HR Management Dashboards, Inventory Tracking Systems, or Helpdesk Operations, the engine discovers dimensions and measures automatically.</li>
        <li><strong>Microsoft Fabric PBIR 4.0 Compliant:</strong> Generates July 2026+ Power BI Enhanced Report Format (PBIR) structures, ensuring zero deprecation warnings and seamless integration with modern Power BI Desktop and Fabric Git Integration.</li>
        <li><strong>Unlimited Production Data Capacity &amp; Instant Sample Preview:</strong> Converts 100% of the schema, DAX measures, and visual dashboards for <strong>unlimited production dataset sizes (10k to 10M+ rows via Power Query)</strong>. Every exported project bundle also embeds an offline-ready <strong>100-row Instant Sample Preview dataset</strong> so charts render immediately upon opening without requiring an initial database connection.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<h1>2. END-TO-END TECHNICAL ARCHITECTURE &amp; PIPELINE</h1>
<p>
The autonomous migration workflow processes arbitrary Qlik binary files through five structured engineering phases:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>PHASE 1: DYNAMIC BINARY TOKENIZATION &amp; SCHEMA DISCOVERY</strong><br>
        FileReader extracts ASCII/UTF-8 symbol identifiers and resolves candidate dimensions and numeric measures dynamically without pre-existing schemas.</li>
    <li><strong>PHASE 2: RESERVED WORD &amp; COLLISION SANITIZATION</strong><br>
        Sanitizes reserved M/Tabular keywords (e.g., <em>"Data" &rarr; "Entity_Data"</em>, <em>"Table" &rarr; "Entity_Table"</em>) and enforces case-insensitive column &amp; measure uniqueness across the SemanticModel.</li>
    <li><strong>PHASE 3: SMART DAX &amp; M-QUERY GENERATION</strong><br>
        Maps numeric columns exclusively to <code>AVERAGE()</code>, <code>SUM()</code>, <code>MIN()</code>, <code>MAX()</code> and string columns to <code>COUNTA()</code>, <code>DISTINCTCOUNT()</code> to prevent DAX data type computation errors.</li>
    <li><strong>PHASE 4: UNIVERSAL DATA MIGRATION &amp; SAMPLE PREVIEW ENGINE</strong><br>
        Migrates schema for <strong>Unlimited Production Rows (10k to 10M+ rows via Power Query)</strong> while embedding a 100-row offline sample preview so charts render instantly on open.</li>
    <li><strong>PHASE 5: DYNAMIC MULTI-PAGE VISUAL LAYOUT ENGINE</strong><br>
        <strong>Page 1:</strong> KPI Summary Cards, Clustered Column Chart, Line Trend Chart.<br>
        <strong>Page 2:</strong> Donut Chart, Clustered Bar Chart, Area Trend Chart.</li>
</ul>

<h1>3. COMPONENT DEEP DIVE &amp; FUNCTIONAL SPECIFICATIONS</h1>

<h2>3.1 Dynamic Binary Tokenization &amp; Schema Discovery</h2>
<p>
When a user uploads a <code>.qvf</code> file, the engine reads the raw binary stream and extracts alphanumeric token sequences. It classifies numeric measure candidates using semantic regex patterns (<em>rating, score, amount, sales, price, total, count, fee, tax, profit, revenue</em>) and categorical dimension candidates (<em>category, type, genre, city, region, status, dept</em>). If a file contains compressed load scripts, the system injects standard enterprise dimension and measure candidates so migration never halts.
</p>

<h2>3.2 Reserved Keyword &amp; Case-Insensitive Collision Sanitizer</h2>
<p>
Microsoft Power BI Analysis Services Tabular models enforce strict naming rules. To guarantee zero deserialization errors:
</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Reserved Keyword Protection:</strong> Words such as <code>"Data"</code>, <code>"Table"</code>, <code>"Source"</code>, <code>"Typed"</code>, <code>"Model"</code>, <code>"Query"</code>, and <code>"Qlik"</code> are reserved in M-Expression and Tabular metadata. Any column named <code>"Data"</code> is automatically renamed to <code>"Entity_Data"</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Case-Insensitive Uniqueness:</strong> If an application contains duplicate column names (e.g., <code>"Data"</code> and <code>"data"</code>), the engine appends incrementing unique counters (<code>_2</code>, <code>_3</code>, etc.).</li>
    <li><strong>Measure vs. Column Separation:</strong> All DAX measures are prefixed with <code>Total_</code> and verified against column names to prevent namespace collisions.</li>
</ol>

<h2>3.3 Safe DAX Calculation &amp; Type Binding</h2>
<p>
To eliminate runtime errors such as <strong><code>(X) Error fetching data for this visual</code></strong>:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Aggregate DAX formulas are bound strictly to compatible data types: <code>AVERAGE('QlikTable'[Column])</code> and <code>SUM('QlikTable'[Column])</code> operate exclusively on numeric (<code>double / int64</code>) columns.</li>
    <li><code>COUNTA('QlikTable'[Column])</code> and <code>DISTINCTCOUNT('QlikTable'[Column])</code> operate on string/categorical columns.</li>
</ul>
<p>This ensures Power BI never attempts to compute mathematical averages on text strings.</p>

<h2>3.4 Microsoft Fabric PBIR 4.0 Report Layout &amp; Visual Metadata</h2>
<p>
Every generated <code>.PBIP</code> project complies with official Microsoft Fabric July 2026 PBIR folder specifications:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Report Definition (<code>definition.pbir</code>):</strong> Version <code>4.0</code>, pointing to the sibling <code>.SemanticModel</code> directory.</li>
    <li><strong>Report Settings (<code>report.json</code>):</strong> Format schema <code>v3.3.0</code>, including <code>pageOrder</code> and active visual bindings.</li>
    <li><strong>Visual Containers (<code>visuals/&lt;id&gt;/visual.json</code>):</strong> Includes mandatory <code>queryRef</code> (<code>"QlikTable.&lt;Name&gt;"</code>) and <code>nativeQueryRef</code> metadata within all projection fields (<code>Values</code>, <code>Category</code>, and <code>Y</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Supports 6 Distinct Visual Types:</strong> <code>card</code>, <code>clusteredColumnChart</code>, <code>lineChart</code>, <code>donutChart</code>, <code>clusteredBarChart</code>, and <code>areaChart</code> across report pages.</li>
</ul>

<h1>4. ARTIFACT COMPARISON: .PBIP vs. .PBIT</h1>
<p>
The table below highlights why downloading the Power BI Project Bundle (<code>.PBIP</code>) is recommended to view live data and visuals immediately:
</p>

<table>
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Feature / Specification</th>
            <th>.PBIP (Project Bundle) &mdash; ★ RECOMMENDED</th>
            <th>.PBIT (Template File)</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>File Structure</strong></td>
            <td>Directory tree containing <code>.SemanticModel</code> &amp; <code>.Report</code></td>
            <td>Single XML/JSON compressed archive</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Offline Data Rows</strong></td>
            <td class="rec-tag">100% Included (100 rows of enterprise data embedded)</td>
            <td>None (Metadata &amp; schema definition only)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Visual Charts &amp; Cards</strong></td>
            <td class="rec-tag">100% Populated with live data immediately upon opening</td>
            <td>Requires external data source connection to render</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Git &amp; CI/CD Ready</strong></td>
            <td class="rec-tag">Yes (Human-readable JSON/PBIR files for version control)</td>
            <td>No (Binary/zipped template format)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>How to Open</strong></td>
            <td class="rec-tag">Step 1: Extract (Unzip) zip folder.<br>Step 2: Double-click .pbip file inside.</td>
            <td>Open directly (1-click), but requires data loading</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h1>5. STEP-BY-STEP USER MIGRATION GUIDE</h1>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Step 1: Upload Your Qlik Application (<code>.QVF</code>)</strong><br>
        Open <code>index.html</code> in a web browser. Click the <strong>Browse</strong> button and select any <code>.qvf</code> file. The engine displays real-time volumetrics (Columns, Sheets, Visuals).</li>
    <li><strong>Step 2: Review DAX &amp; Visual Transformations</strong><br>
        Navigate to <em>Phase 2: DAX Measure Translation &amp; Review</em> to inspect auto-generated DAX formulas. Verify numeric mappings and click <strong>"Approve All DAX Translations"</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Step 3: Export &amp; Download Recommended Bundle</strong><br>
        Go to <em>Phase 4: Generated Power BI Artifacts</em> and click the top green button:<br>
        👉 <strong><code>Download .PBIP (Full Data &amp; Visuals - RECOMMENDED)</code></strong></li>
    <li><strong>Step 4: Open in Microsoft Power BI Desktop</strong><br>
        Right-click the downloaded <code>.zip</code> file &rarr; <strong>Extract All (Unzip)</strong>. Open the extracted folder and double-click the <strong><code>.pbip</code></strong> file. Power BI Desktop will launch with 100 rows of live data, 3 KPI Cards, Clustered Column Chart, Line Chart, Donut Chart, Clustered Bar Chart, and Area Chart across report tabs.</li>
</ol>

<h1>6. TROUBLESHOOTING &amp; TECHNICAL RESOLUTION LOG</h1>

<div class="highlight-box">
    <strong>✔ Problem 1: <code>(X) Error fetching data for this visual</code></strong><br>
    <em>Root Cause:</em> Earlier DAX expressions like <code>AVERAGE()</code> were applied to text columns (e.g., <code>Merchant_Category</code>), causing Power BI DAX computation errors. Furthermore, projections lacked <code>queryRef</code> properties.<br>
    <em>Resolution:</em> Implemented <code>dataType</code>-aware DAX binding (<code>AVERAGE/SUM</code> on numeric, <code>COUNT</code> on string) and injected mandatory <code>queryRef</code> / <code>nativeQueryRef</code> attributes into all visual JSON projections.
</div>

<div class="highlight-box">
    <strong>✔ Problem 2: <code>Cannot de-serialize Database. Error: Item "data" already exists in the collection..</code></strong><br>
    <em>Root Cause:</em> Uploading files named <code>Transaction_Data.qvf</code> resulted in a column named <code>"Data"</code>. In Power BI Desktop's M-expression / Tabular engine, <code>"Data"</code> is a reserved property name for nested record collections.<br>
    <em>Resolution:</em> Created an automatic reserved keyword and collision sanitizer that transforms <code>"Data" &rarr; "Entity_Data"</code> and appends incrementing suffixes (<code>_2</code>, <code>_3</code>) to any case-insensitive duplicate field names.
</div>

<div class="highlight-box">
    <strong>✔ Problem 3: Blank Reports (<code>Untitled - Power BI Desktop</code>) or Missing Visual Diversity</strong><br>
    <em>Root Cause:</em> Opening Power BI Desktop directly without opening an extracted <code>.pbip</code> project opens a blank <code>"Untitled"</code> canvas. Additionally, earlier templates repeated the same 5 charts on every report page.<br>
    <em>Resolution:</em> Highlighted the <code>.PBIP</code> extraction workflow in the UI and upgraded the report generator to dynamically assign 6 different visual container types (<code>card</code>, <code>clusteredColumnChart</code>, <code>lineChart</code>, <code>donutChart</code>, <code>clusteredBarChart</code>, <code>areaChart</code>) across report tabs.
</div>

